.TH READ 1
.SH NAME
read \- read a line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B read
[
.B \-m
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Read
copies to standard output exactly one line from the standard input.
It is useful in
.IR rc (1)
scripts, as
.BR "x=`{read}" .
.PP
The
.B \-m
flag causes it to continue reading and writing until end of file.
.PP
When reading a line,
.I read
takes one byte at a time from its input,
so it never consumes more than it prints.
A descriptor shared with the shell is left at the start of the next line,
whether the input is a console, which hands over whole lines,
or a pipe, which may not.
The line is written with its newline, or without one when the input
ends first.
.SH SOURCE
.B cmd/read/main.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cat (1),
.IR rc (1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Read
exits with status
.B eof
when there is nothing to read.
.SH BUGS
Unlike Plan 9's
.IR read ,
this one takes no file arguments and has no
.BR \-n ,
.B \-c
or
.BR \-r .
